WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.660
- [Narrator] Have you ever wondered

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.110
what would it be like to
taste food for a living?

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.120
Well, that's what these people do.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.210
They're called Sensory Evaluators.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.320
You might call them taste testers,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.070
but they're so much more than that.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.130
- My name is Gail Vance Civille.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.380
I am the founder, owner, and
president of Sensory Spectrum,

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.720
and I have been in the sensory
business for over 50 years.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.400
You do the math.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.070
- [Narrator] Sensory
evaluators are trained

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.670
for over 100 hours.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:30.900
More on that later.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.140
They evaluate the texture, flavor,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.750
or fragrance of a product.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.760
From toilet paper to chocolate
to cheese, they test it all.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.240
Why do sensory evaluators do what they do?

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.862
- Very often our job is
to look at prototypes

00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:48.030
with some ingredient substituted,

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.560
so that we can tell
the product developers,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.360
yes you are safe making this

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.970
in the place of your current product,

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.170
and still having the
same sensory properties.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.730
- [Narrator] There's a
whole host of reasons

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.360
why a product might change.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.630
Like swapping out an ingredient

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.500
for something cheaper or healthier.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.860
Take for instance, Kraft Mac &amp; Cheese.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.720
Remember when they changed
to all natural ingredients?

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.090
No preservatives or artificial dyes,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.550
but the flavor stayed exactly the same.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.383
Boom!

00:01:16.383 --> 00:01:18.710
Thank your sensory evaluators.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.742
- The role of sensory evaluation

00:01:20.742 --> 00:01:25.390
is to stand next to the
product developer and say,

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.470
if you have a charge
to make a new product,

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.350
to make an improved product,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.890
to make an ingredient substitution,

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.320
I am here to tell you
whether or not you succeeded.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.310
Were you able to maintain
the appearance, the flavor,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.180
and the texture of that product.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.270
That is my job.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.970
I stand there and say, you know what,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.120
this is very, very close,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.440
or it's spot on, I think you
should move forward with it.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.440
- [Narrator] So how would
Gail describe cheese?

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.030
- Cheese.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.080
There's either sheep, cow, or goat dairy,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.420
so there's milk and then there's milk fat,

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.190
and then there are the fermented
notes that are in the milk.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.880
So let's take cheddar cheese,
which is a cow milk cheese,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.640
that has milky notes,
milk-fat notes, nutty notes,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.430
skunky notes, sometimes fecal,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.243
or as we like to describe
with the technical term, poop.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.670
And it actually belongs there,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.240
when you have farmhouse cheddar cheeses

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.240
from England or Scotland or Wales,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.170
or some places in Australia,

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.360
you will have a fecal
note that belongs there.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.340
And then you also will have, potentially,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.990
some fruity notes as well,

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.410
and salty, sweet, sour, and bitter,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.340
and then you have all the textures,

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.590
which is the hardness and the melt,

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.070
and the chewiness or cohesiveness,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.640
and how fast it disappears in your mouth,

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.670
and whether or not it leaves a fatty film.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.420
- [Narrator] To evaluate
a new or altered product,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.210
they gather a panel.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.560
Each panelist is handed a sample.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.930
In this case, pasta sauce.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.000
They'll taste the sauce

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.500
and evaluate its flavor,
texture, and smell.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.920
Then they rate each attribute on a scale.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.094
- Bitter?

00:03:12.094 --> 00:03:12.927
- [Panelist] Zero.

00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:13.843
- Zero.
- One.

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:14.798
- Zero.
- Two.

00:03:14.798 --> 00:03:16.730
- Zero.
- One.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:18.730
- [Narrator] There's
an entire book of terms

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.176
to describe different foods.

00:03:20.176 --> 00:03:21.520
Gail wrote it.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.353
- Brassy.

00:03:22.353 --> 00:03:24.290
Minerally flinty, if you will.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.790
Sweet and sour.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.350
Milky notes, nutty notes, skunky notes.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.940
- [Narrator] How do
these panelists identify

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.940
how salty or sweet something is?

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.910
During training,

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.878
they're exposed to
different levels of flavor.

00:03:35.878 --> 00:03:39.000
A two salt is very mildly salty,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.193
while a 20 salt is closer
to ocean water, and so on.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.130
Panelists learn these until
they're second nature.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.970
- The whole idea of being a great panelist

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.340
is to never think about what you're doing.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.450
Just do it, like a hockey goalie.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.830
Just catch the puck.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.110
You're no longer mentally processing.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.460
When I taste, I literally
feel like the sample

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:05.930
goes into my mouth,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.630
and then the sample tells me about itself.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:09.560
- [Narrator] How has this training

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.560
affected sensory evaluators
in their daily lives?

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.040
- I'm not a normal consumer.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.380
I am not normal.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:16.480
I am very picky.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.440
If I'm in a restaurant
where a lot of people

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.950
are wearing fragrance,
I will not enjoy it.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.830
So it has jaded me or
poisoned me or corrupted me

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:30.430
to being really obsessively picky about

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:34.270
what I'm eating, where I'm
eating, and when I'm eating.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.740
- [Narrator] For better or worse,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.907
actually tasting what you
eat may be a good thing.

